AB  - Large language models are attractive in financial services because they can turn dense policy text, complaint narratives, and public filings into faster answers for analysts. The problem is that the same systems can also be manipulated, leak sensitive content, or sound confident when they are wrong. This paper presents a practical guardrail architecture for LLM-assisted financial decision support and evaluates it in a small but reproducible pilot built on public U.S. regulatory and consumer-finance sources. The prototype uses role-aware retrieval, prompt-injection screening, output redaction, evidence checks, and safe abstention. To keep the evaluation reproducible across institutions, the experiment isolates the guardrail plane with a local retrieval-and-policy harness over public financial text derived from CFPB, SEC, FinCEN, FFIEC, NIST, PCI DSS, and NYDFS materials. On an evaluation set of 18 in scope questions, 20 prompt-injection attacks, 20 leakage probes, and 12 out-of-scope prompts, the guarded system preserved in scope grounded accuracy at 94.4%, reduced prompt-injection success from 100% to 0%, reduced leakage from 100% to 0%, and lowered unsupported answers on out-of-scope prompts from 100% to 16.7%. The results are from a pilot prototype rather than a production bank deployment, but they show that useful financial AI systems become far more trustworthy when the control plane is engineered as seriously as the model itself.
