AB  - Legal question answering over the Indian Penal Code 1860 (IPC) demands precision, structured reasoning, and source verifiability. This work identifies two failure modes in LLM-based IPC retrieval. First, multi-turn context degradation: LLM accuracy declines substantially across extended dialogue as earlier legal context is progressively displaced within the context window. Second, corpus context drift: all four evaluated LLMs spontaneously cite Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023 (BNS) section numbers when answering IPC-targeted queries despite explicit IPC-only instructions—an out-of-scope hallucination caused by LLMs mapping to the current statutory code.We propose a Multi-Agent Graph RAG Framework comprising: (i) a typed Knowledge Graph of 598 nodes and 15,430 relational edges from 575 IPC sections; (ii) TF-IDF sparse retrieval for jargon-precise section identification; and (iii) a ReAct-driven Agent-to-Agent (A2A) protocol with three parallel specialised agents (Researcher, Prosecutor, Defense).Evaluation on 30 LLM baseline queries finds Full Section Accuracy of 63.3–98.3%, with every LLM producing BNS citations for 10 queries. An ablation study over 100 queries (10 turns × 10 queries per turn) shows the Knowledge Graph contributes +8.0 pp Hit@5 (p=0.013) and EXCEPTION_OF edges +7.0 pp (p=0.023). The Multi-Agent A2A protocol contributes 0 pp to retrieval coverage but improves qualitative answer structure. The framework achieves 94% overall multi-turn accuracy, 100% T10 retention, and zero BNS corpus drift across 100 queries.
