AB  - Radiology reports are a critical source of clinical information but are often lengthy, variable in structure, and difficult for non-radiologists to navigate. Question answering (QA) systems can help clinicians rapidly retrieve relevant findings, yet existing evaluations of extractive QA on radiology reports rely heavily on aggregate metrics such as exact match (EM) and F1, which obscure important failure modes. In this work, we benchmark modern open large language models (LLMs), such as LLaMA 3.1-8B, LLaMA 3.2-3B, and Mistral-Small-22B, on the RadQA dataset under zero-shot, few-shot, and fine-tuned settings. We report performance using EM, F1, and a sentence-level measure (SM), and introduce a structured error taxonomy that disaggregates incorrect predictions into eight categories, including hallucination, superset, subset, and abstention errors. Our results show that fine-tuning substantially outperforms in-context prompting, with LLaMA 3.1-8B achieving 61% EM, 77% F1, and 85% SM, surpassing previously reported baselines on RadQA. The taxonomy reveals that fine-tuning not only raises accuracy but also eliminates hallucinations and reduces false positive and false negative abstentions, concentrating residual errors in boundary-level categories such as superset and subset. These findings demonstrate the value of combining standard metrics with taxonomy-driven analysis to better understand model behavior and highlight future directions, including reasoning-oriented LLMs and hybrid extractive–generative approaches, for building reliable clinical QA systems.
