AB  - Although Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate impressive success in open-ended text generation, their propensity for hallucination limits their utility in knowledge-intensive and high-reliability applications. This study proposes a self-contained, agentic, and self-verifying Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system designed for Turkish knowledge-based question-answering. The developed architecture comprises a five-stage pipeline: dynamic routing, multi-query generation, content relevance filtering, citation-based response generation, and groundedness verification. The primary contribution of this work is an abstention mechanism that integrates thresholding with LLM-based sufficiency evaluation to prevent the system from generating erroneous information when sources are insufficient or irrelevant. Experimental evaluation conducted on a Turkish knowledge base consisting of 118 documents in the artificial intelligence domain and a 45-question test suite demonstrates a 100% routing accuracy, an MRR of 0.90 in retrieval performance, and a 100% groundedness rate, while achieving 100% correct abstention and 0% hallucination risk on out-of-domain questions. These findings indicate that the proposed self-verified and agentic RAG approach offers a reliable and verifiable information retrieval framework for the Turkish language.
