AB  - Commissioning an industrial field device still requires an automation engineer to locate the correct manual, extract dozens of protocol-specific parameters (register maps, node identifiers, station addresses, baud rates), and transcribe them into a supervisory system, a workflow that is slow, expensive, and vulnerable to transcription errors whose consequences range from silent data loss to unsafe actuator behavior. This paper presents CLARITY (Context-aware LLM-Assisted Retrieval and Intelligent Auto-configuraTion sYstem), a fully on-premises pipeline that automates this workflow for Modbus, OPC UA, Profibus DP, and CANopen devices. CLARITY chunks device manuals into a hybrid knowledge base combining 1024-dimensional BGE-M3 dense embeddings, BM25 sparse retrieval, and an ontology-graph term boost fused by Reciprocal Rank Fusion; reranks candidates with a sigmoid-normalized cross-encoder; guards a local llama3.1:8b generator with a two-stage abstention gate (rerank-score threshold and IRI resolution ratio); validates generated JSON-LD configurations against protocol-specific SHACL shapes; and deploys through protocol adapters with post-deployment read-back verification. We contribute a component-wise evaluation methodology with evidence-grounded relevance labels and release the full harness. On a 71-chunk corpus with 28 field-level queries, hybrid retrieval with reranking reaches HitRate@10 of 0.964 and MRR@10 of 0.625; rerank scores of documented and undocumented queries separate by a factor of 8.6 with no overlap, so a wide threshold band yields perfect abstention decisions. The generator attains micro field-level F1 = 0.867 with a systematic failure isolated to the OPC UA schema, a defect that component-wise evaluation localizes to the generator and its validation shape rather than retrieval, and that read-back verification catches in every run. Across 15 end-to-end runs on an H100 GPU (a deliberately small benchmark, whose success rates we therefore report with 95% Wilson confidence intervals), documented devices are commissioned in 2.6 to 6.6 seconds with no unsafe writes (0/15; upper 95% bound 0.20) and every failing run terminating in an explicit, machine-readable failure signal (7/7 detected). Modbus and OPC UA are exercised over live network I/O with read-back, whereas Profibus DP and CANopen are validated structurally without bus I/O; all generation uses a single local open-weights model (llama3.1:8b); holding retrieval and validation fixed and swapping only the generator, four open on-premises models all attain field-level F1 between 0.83 and 0.97, indicating the accuracy is a property of the pipeline rather than of one model. A case study then applies the pipeline to a Universal Robots UR5e collaborative robot, with the vendor’s published manuals as the only corpus and a physics-simulated UR5e behind the vendor’s documented Modbus register map as a live deployment target: all three commissioning runs deploy the correct four-register configuration with field-level F1 = 1.0 and pass sentinel, read-back, physical-consistency, and liveness checks, while retrieval quality drops sharply on the vendor’s table-heavy documents and the benchmark-calibrated abstention threshold does not transfer, requiring recalibration from the measured score separation of the new corpus. We argue that this defense-in-depth safety profile, correct behavior of the whole despite an imperfect generator, is the property industrial adoption actually requires, and we characterize its cost, latency, and limits.
