AB  - This paper presents a controlled evaluation of three reasoning strategies for vision-guided robotic manipulation: YOLO-only rule-based heuristics, Large Language Model (LLM) symbolic planning, and a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) planner. All methods use the same perception and control pipeline, with YOLO for object detection and a Franka Panda robot for execution. This setup isolates the impact of reasoning. We evaluate the planners on fifteen structured test scenarios covering phrasing variation, constraint following, ambiguity, abstention, recovery, and edge cases, for 150 episodes per planner and 450 total execution episodes. The RAG-enhanced planner achieves 98 percent task success, full policy compliance, zero unsafe actions, and a 92 percent reduction in retries compared to the baseline. All planners produce identical symbolic policies, showing that performance gains come from more robust execution rather than changes in plan generation. These results demonstrate that retrieval-augmented reasoning improves the reliability of language-driven robotic manipulation.
