AB  - Colleges are experiencing an increased need to have smart, multi-lingual support systems that can respond to various questions posed by the students. Although large language models (LLMs) have strong general capabilities, they are often inaccurate, culturally insensitive, and unsafe in low-resource and domain-specific settings. The paper introduces a two-stage fine-tuning model for developing a multilingual student services assistant specializing in English, Modern Standard Arabic (MSA), Franco Arabic, and Omani Arabic. Its purpose is to improve the factual accuracy, dialogue coherence, and refusal accuracy in the sensitive institutional environment. In Phase I, factual memorization was performed by fine-tuning the TyDi QA and MASSIVE datasets with LoRA adapters to enhance multilingual understanding. Phase II used behavioral alignment, including institutional data and the presence of refusal-inducing prompts, to improve the safe management of out-of-scope queries. The evaluation metrics were factual accuracy, BLEU, COMET, refusal precision, and multi-turn coherence, with performance evaluated against GPT-4, Aya-Base, and Vicuna. The suggested model was more successful in refusal accuracy (0.86 vs. GPT-4 at 0.82) and competitive accuracy (0.78 vs. GPT-4 at 0.81). It is noteworthy that the dialectal performance was enhanced, especially in the Omani Arabic. Domain-specific finetuning can be used to create safer and more inclusive multilingual LLM assistants in higher education and find the balance between accuracy and reliability between underrepresented languages.
